Yes, you can give Claude the Confluence page HTML, but raw Confluence inner HTML is not the best authoring format. For an architectural/security vision page, I recommend using a structured intermediate format and only converting back to Confluence HTML at the end.

Recommended workflow

Confluence Page
      ↓
Copy HTML
      ↓
Claude extracts structure/content
      ↓
Architecture + Security Vision model
      ↓
Claude updates/restructures
      ↓
Confluence-compatible HTML
      ↓
Copy/paste back to Confluence

Best approach: preserve a structured document model

Ask Claude to first convert the existing page into:

1. Purpose & Vision
2. Business Context
3. Architecture Principles
4. Target Architecture
5. Processing Model
6. Data & Integration Architecture
7. Security Architecture
8. Identity & Access
9. Data Protection
10. Network Security
11. Secrets & Key Management
12. Audit & Observability
13. Resilience / Multi-DC
14. Failure & Recovery
15. Deployment Architecture
16. Operational Model
17. Risks & Decisions
18. Architecture Roadmap

Then provide your new architectural requirements and ask Claude to merge them without losing existing content.

Don’t ask Claude simply:

“Update this HTML.”

Instead use a prompt like:

You are an enterprise architecture and security documentation assistant.

I will provide:

1. Existing Confluence page HTML.
2. New architecture requirements and decisions.
3. A predefined documentation structure/template.

Your task is to update the existing architecture page while preserving valuable existing content.

Rules:

* Preserve the existing architectural intent unless explicitly superseded.
* Do not invent architecture decisions, technologies, security controls, compliance requirements, or implementation details.
* Clearly distinguish CURRENT STATE, TARGET STATE, DECISION, ASSUMPTION, and OPEN QUESTION.
* Remove obsolete or duplicated content when the new design supersedes it.
* Keep terminology consistent throughout the document.
* Make architecture relationships explicit.
* Include security considerations for authentication, authorization, encryption, secrets, data protection, network controls, audit, logging, vulnerability management, and least privilege.
* Include resilience considerations for multi-instance, multi-DC, failure recovery, retry, idempotency, ownership/lease, and disaster recovery.
* For every major architectural component, document responsibility, dependencies, trust boundaries, failure behavior, and security controls.
* Do not expose credentials, tokens, secrets, internal sensitive values, or unnecessary implementation details.

Output TWO sections:

SECTION 1 — REVIEW
Provide:

* Existing sections retained
* Sections changed
* New sections
* Removed/replaced content
* Open questions
* Architectural/security gaps

SECTION 2 — FINAL CONFLUENCE CONTENT
Produce the complete updated page using the supplied template.

The final output must be directly usable in Confluence.

Use:

* Clear headings
* Tables where appropriate
* Architecture decision tables
* Mermaid diagrams where supported
* Concise bullet points
* Consistent terminology
* Security and operational callouts

Do not return partial HTML fragments. Return the complete updated page.

EXISTING CONFLUENCE HTML:
<<>>

DOCUMENT TEMPLATE:
<<>>

NEW ARCHITECTURE/SECURITY REQUIREMENTS:
<<>>

Even better: use Markdown as the working format

If your Confluence page isn’t extremely formatting-heavy, I’d have Claude produce:

Confluence HTML
        ↓
Claude
        ↓
Canonical Markdown
        ↓
Architecture review/update
        ↓
Canonical Markdown
        ↓
Confluence HTML

This is much easier to maintain than repeatedly asking an AI to manipulate nested Confluence HTML.

For example, keep the authoritative version in Git:

docs/
 └── architecture/
     └── ach-processing-architecture.md

Then generate/paste the Confluence representation from that document.

